# secrets & env
.env
*.key
# Signing keys are operator-managed, out of band, and never committed (rule 4, rule 10): the Ed25519
# audit keypair (audit-signing-key.pem / audit-public-key.pem) and any HS256 token-key material.
*.pem
guardian-signing-key.hex

# python
__pycache__/
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# experiment data (backed up per data-management protocol, not via git)
runs/

# Monitor replay output. scripts/monitor_replay.sh writes each fixture's emitted event stream here
# so it can be diffed against the tracked golden output under monitor/fixtures/<id>/expected/. The
# emitted copy is a build product of the golden one and must never be tracked: a committed copy is
# a second answer to "what does this fixture produce", and the first time the two disagree the
# repository holds a golden file that no longer describes the aggregator. The determinism check
# (M2-T2.7) writes its two runs here as well, for the same reason.
monitor/.replay/

# Live Monitor source bytes and emitted events. kind mounts this directory into the control-plane
# for the Kubernetes audit log and the live aggregator persists webhook inputs beneath it. These are
# captured run data, never authored fixtures; promotion into monitor/fixtures/ requires explicit
# provenance after a live run, so the runtime directory itself must not become an accidental fixture.
monitor/runtime/evidence/

# Langfuse volumes. docs/04-repo-conventions.md section 5 requires these ignored, and this rule was
# deferred deliberately rather than overlooked: until M4 no such directory existed, and the note
# that stood here promised the pattern would be "added in the same change that creates the volumes,
# so that the ignore rule and the thing it ignores are reviewed together". M4 is that change. The
# Compose stack at infra/observability/langfuse/ brings up Langfuse v3 with its Postgres,
# ClickHouse, Redis and MinIO, and every one of those services binds its state under the directory
# below — trace payloads, an object store and two databases, which are run output rather than
# repository content and are regenerated by bringing the stack up again. The rule is anchored to
# that one path rather than written as a bare `volumes/`, because an unanchored pattern would
# silently ignore any directory of that name anywhere in the tree, including one a later milestone
# means to track. Every other path section 5 names is covered above.
infra/observability/langfuse/volumes/

# CVE mirror tarballs. scenarios/cve/images.yaml pins a digest per known-vulnerable image the CVE
# scenarios admit, and a digest pin buys immutability, not availability: if the registry drops the
# manifest, the digest resolves to nothing. The mechanism that survives deletion is a local mirror —
# `docker save` per image, `kind load image-archive` into the cluster — and it is the one mechanism
# git cannot hold, because each of those tarballs is hundreds of megabytes of container image. So
# the repository carries a verifiable *recipe* for the mirror rather than the mirror: the commands
# in scenarios/cve/README.md, one `tarball_sha256` per entry in images.yaml, and
# `sh scripts/cve_mirror.sh --verify` to re-hash what a machine actually holds against what the
# corpus recorded. The checksum is what makes the recipe verifiable instead of merely written down.
# The rule is anchored to the full path rather than written as a bare `tarballs/` on the Langfuse
# block's own argument directly above: an unanchored pattern would silently ignore any directory of
# that name anywhere in the tree, including one a later milestone means to track. Added in the same
# change that creates the directory it ignores, so the pattern and the thing it ignores are reviewed
# together.
scenarios/cve/tarballs/

# Scenario determinism replay output. scripts/scenario_determinism.sh plans the whole
# (scenario, injection-condition, seed) matrix twice, into scenarios/.replay/determinism/run-1/ and
# run-2/, and compares the two trees cell by cell with cmp. Those plans are a build product of the
# planner and the corpus and must never be tracked, for the reason monitor/.replay/ gives above: a
# committed copy is a second answer to "what does this cell plan", and the first time it disagrees
# with what the planner now produces, the repository holds a determinism artefact that no longer
# describes the harness. Anchored to the full path rather than a bare `.replay/`, on the same
# argument as the two blocks above.
scenarios/.replay/

# The synthetic-analysis proof sentinel. runner/synthetic.py generates a synthetic run corpus and
# analysis/render.py, on a successful `make analyze` over it, writes this file recording the git
# commit, generator seed, corpus digest and artefact list -- the pre-registration lock the
# `make campaign` preflight reads (ADR-042). It is GENERATED, not authored: committing it would let
# the gate pass on a tree that never ran the proof, since the gate's whole check is that the
# recorded commit matches the tree about to produce real data. The analyze outputs themselves land
# under runs/<campaign>/_analysis/, already ignored by the runs/ rule above. Anchored to the one
# path rather than a bare glob, on the argument the blocks above give.
analysis/.synthetic-proof.json

# Local calibration scratch. `.calib/` holds Locust CSV exports and working files from
# local calibration runs. They are generated measurement artefacts, not repository content:
# values that survive calibration are recorded through the calibration commit into the
# protocol and limit files, the same treatment `runs/` and the replay directories above get.
.calib/

# Supply-chain self-scan output (M8-T8.4). scripts/self_scan.sh writes each image's CycloneDX
# SBOM plus its full and gate Trivy reports here; the CI workflow (.github/workflows/self-scan.yml)
# uploads them as build artefacts instead. They are generated scan output that changes with every
# vulnerability-database update, not repository content -- a committed copy would be a second,
# instantly-stale answer to "what does our image contain", the same argument the replay and proof
# blocks above give. Accepted residual findings live in the tracked ignore file (docs/ops/sbom.md),
# never in a committed report. Anchored to the full path rather than a bare glob, as above.
self-scan-out/
